# Unmix the signal: FastICA from scratch

Blind audio source separation with Independent Component Analysis. Centering, PCA whitening (covariance + eigendecomposition) and FastICA are all implemented in NumPy. There is no library ICA or PCA.

Results, plots and the answers to the four "be ready to explain" questions are in [REPORT.md](REPORT.md).

## Audio

| | files |
|---|---|
| Clean sources | [speech](data/sources/speech.wav), [music](data/sources/music.wav), [ambient](data/sources/ambient.wav) |
| Mixtures (3 mics) | [mix 1](outputs/mixtures/mixture_1.wav), [mix 2](outputs/mixtures/mixture_2.wav), [mix 3](outputs/mixtures/mixture_3.wav) |
| ICA output | [speech](outputs/separated/ica_speech.wav), [music](outputs/separated/ica_music.wav), [ambient](outputs/separated/ica_ambient.wav) |
| PCA-only (for comparison) | [speech](outputs/pca_only/pca_speech.wav), [music](outputs/pca_only/pca_music.wav), [ambient](outputs/pca_only/pca_ambient.wav) |

The sources are generated by `scripts/make_sources.py`: espeak-ng speech, synthesized music, synthesized rain. No third-party audio is used.

## Headline numbers

Mean SI-SDR 44.8 dB for ICA, 1.4 dB for PCA alone, about 0 dB for the raw mixtures. Per-source correlation with the originals is 0.9999 or higher.

## Layout

```
ica/
  preprocessing.py   center(), pca_whiten()
  fastica.py         fastica(): symmetric + deflation, logcosh / exp / cube
  metrics.py         correlation, Hungarian matching, SI-SDR, Amari index
  pipeline.py        mixing matrix, noise, separate() = center -> whiten -> FastICA
  audio_io.py        WAV read/write
scripts/
  make_sources.py    generate the three clean sources
  run_ica.py         main run: mix, separate, export audio, metrics, plots
  experiments.py     PCA vs ICA, SNR sweep, non-linearities, Gaussianity, #mixtures
tests/test_core.py   whitening, orthogonality, recovery for every variant, SI-SDR
data/sources/        clean sources
outputs/             mixtures, separated audio, plots, metrics.json, experiments.json
```

## Run it

### Quick start (all platforms — sources already included)
```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
python scripts/run_ica.py             # about 1 s — mixes, separates, evaluates, exports audio + plots
python scripts/experiments.py         # about 1-2 min — full experiment suite
python tests/test_core.py             # unit tests
```

### Regenerate sources (Linux/macOS only)
```bash
sudo apt install espeak-ng            # or: brew install espeak-ng
python scripts/make_sources.py        # generates data/sources/*.wav
```
> **Windows:** `make_sources.py` requires `espeak-ng` which isn't available natively. The repo already includes the generated sources in `data/sources/`, so you can skip this step. Use WSL or provide your own WAVs.

Options for `run_ica.py`: `--n-mix 4` (more mixtures than sources), `--algorithm deflation`, `--fun exp|cube|logcosh`, `--seed`.

To use your own sources, put three clean mono WAVs named `speech.wav`, `music.wav` and `ambient.wav` (same sample rate) into `data/sources/`.

To run on real multi-device recordings (one WAV per device, time-aligned, number of devices equal to the number of sources):

```
python scripts/run_ica.py --mixtures-dir path/to/recordings --out outputs_real
```

This has no ground truth, so it writes `component_k.wav` files and a waveform plot but no metrics. It also assumes instantaneous mixing, so expect weaker results with real rooms (see Limitations in the report).

## Known gaps

Real-world recordings and the mini-Shazam stretch goal are not done. Mixing is instantaneous only.
